  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00001">Jada received a gift of $180. In the first week, she spent a third of the gift money. She continues spending a third of what is left each week thereafter.</para>
    
    <para id="para-00002">Which equation best represents the amount of gift money \(g\), in dollars, she has after \(t\)<emphasis> </emphasis>weeks?</para>
    <para id="para-00003">\(g=180−\frac{1}{3}t\)</para>
      <para id="para-00004">\(g=180 \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^t\)</para>
      <para id="para-00005">\(g=\frac{1}{3} \cdot 180^t\)</para>
      <para id="para-00006">\(g=180 \cdot (\frac{2}{3})^t\)</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00007">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00008">
      \(g=180 \cdot (\frac{2}{3})^t\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00009">Explain your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00010">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00001">
      <item>Jada's initial gift is $180. Then, she spends a third \(\frac{1}{3}\) of the whole amount each week. This means that she has \(\frac{2}{3}\) left after each week because \(1 - \frac{1}{3} = \frac{2}{3} \) (the whole amount minus a third).</item>
      <item>When writing the equation, \(a = 180 \) because that is the starting amount she was given. Then, because she spends \(\frac{1}{3}\) each week, that means only \(\frac{2}{3}\) is leftover. so, \(b = \frac{2}{3}\). The equation that results is \(g = 180 \cdot (23)^t\).</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content></document>